Part 3: Common Interview Questions for Two Sum

Q1. What is the brute-force approach?
The brute-force approach is to check every possible pair of numbers.
For each element, compare it with every remaining element.
If their sum equals the target, return the indices.

Complexity
Time
O(n²)

Space
O(1)
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Why is brute force inefficient?
Because we compare every pair.
If there are n elements,
the total comparisons are approximately
n × n
which results in
O(n²)
For large arrays, this becomes slow.
-----------------------------------

Q3. What optimization can we make?
Instead of searching the entire array repeatedly,
store previously visited numbers in a HashMap.
This allows constant-time lookup.
-----------------------------------------------

Q4. Why HashMap?
HashMap provides approximately
O(1)
lookup time.
Instead of searching the array,
we directly check whether the complement already exists.
------------------------------------------------------

Q5. What is Complement?
Complement is the number required to reach the target.
Formula
complement = target - currentNumber;
Example
Target = 9
Current = 2
Complement = 7
So instead of asking
Which two numbers add to 9?
We ask
Does 7 already exist?
------------------------------------------------------

Q6. Why do we calculate the complement?
Because finding the required number is easier than checking every possible pair.
We reduce unnecessary comparisons.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Why do we store after checking?
To avoid matching an element with itself.
Example
[3,3]
Target = 6
If we store first,
the first 3 could immediately match itself
Checking first ensures we only match with previously seen elements.
------------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Why use unordered_map instead of map in C++?
unordered_map
Average Lookup
O(1)
map
Lookup
O(log n)
Since ordering isn't needed,
unordered_map is faster.
------------------------------------------------

Q9. What does the HashMap store?
It stores
Number → Index
Example
7 → 3
Meaning
Number 7 is located at index 3.
-------------------------------

Q10. Why store the index instead of only the value?
Because the problem asks us to return indices.
When the complement is found,
we already know where it occurred.
-----------------------------------------------------

Q11. Can duplicate values exist?
Yes.
Example
3 3
The HashMap still works because each element is processed separately, and the current value is added only after checking for its complement.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q12. Why does the algorithm only need one loop?
Because every number is visited once.
The complement lookup is constant time.
Therefore,
one traversal is enough.
----------------------------------------------

Q13. Time Complexity?
Loop
O(n)
Lookup
O(1)
Overall
O(n)
---------------------

Q14. Space Complexity?
Worst case,
all elements are stored.
O(n)
-----------------------------

Q15. What if no answer exists?
On LeetCode,
the problem guarantees exactly one solution.
In a general implementation,
we could return an empty vector or indicate that no pair was found.

